this week the Trump administration  lifted a ban on the importation of some  trophies from animals hunted for sport  that includes elephant parts despite  statements from the president in  November that such a practice is quote a  horror show the US Fish and Wildlife  Service says it will make determinations  on whether to allow trophy imports on a  case-by-case basis you're going to tell  us more about the new policy and what it  means is dr. M sanjayan CEO of  Conservation International he is also  the executive producer of the new  virtual reality film my Africa which  debuts at the Tribeca Film Festival next  month  sangeun good morning good morning what  does this mean case by case basis what  are the implications for the effort  elephant population we actually don't  know and that's the problem with this  right it's more uncertainty just when we  felt like we were making some real  progress with ending the poaching of  large-scale poaching of elephants in  Africa just when China in January  announced that it's banning its domestic  market for ivory this new factor comes  in and creates uncertainty around how we  manage elephant populations let's let's  talk about the risk to elephants because  they're in grave danger correct  absolutely yes how significant well so  when I was a child when I was in high  school they were probably about a  million 1.2 million elephants in Africa  today there may be about 300,000 the  great global elephant survey comes up  with it's hard to know exactly so the  population has declined it's declined  across the range you know some countries  are doing well and we're seeing the tide  turn the price of ivory is dramatically  dropped from you know two thousand  dollars a kilo you know four years ago  to about four hundred dollars a kilo  today well but just when this is  happening just when we have moral  leadership on this issue when the United  States from the UK when now China can  take some leadership on this issue  this new uncertainty has created a level  of anxiety amongst people who really  care about elephants the president  called this kind of hunting a horror  show and he was probably right and he  has been sort of unstinting at least  publicly in his criticism of it yet this  is happening we're back at this issue  who's driving this I don't know I mean  look I was shocked by this  as you were because President Trump was  crystal clear on this in November not  just tweeting but publicly made  statements after story you did a New  York Times didn't others you know what  was unusual about this new announcement  that there's some backtracking of this  is case by case allowing of trophy  hunting for elephants to be brought back  into this country that announcement was  made by Safari Club International which  in itself is a little unusual we expect  impartiality amongst our agencies like  US Fish and Wildlife Service that they  should be determined by science so  having a organization private  organization announced this is a bit  unnerving just for people who don't know  what is safari club and it's it's  basically an organization that supports  trophy hunting of sort of in primarily  big game species around the world and  they were making the announcement on  this that's how we learned about it do  we have any information on what the  guidelines actually are gonna be for  these judgments and and who's gonna be  essentially judging it no we don't have  any clear understanding of what these  guidelines are gonna be or how you're  gonna judge it now understand that  hunting has been used as a conservation  tool frankly I'm a hunter myself right  and in this country hunting is used as a  very strong conservation tool but even  in the United States we don't hunt  endangered species if we don't hunt  endangered species here why do we think  it's okay to hunt endangered species in  Zambia or Zimbabwe when clearly the  regulations are far more obscure what  needs to be done to ensure the survival  of elephants what can ordinary Americans  people watching the show do how can  people participate in ensuring their  survival well don't buy anything that  has ivory on it that's simple and it's  illegal now and it should be that way  and and then get your voice heard you  know make your voice heard we want the  US Fish and Wildlife Service to have an  open process where public can comment on  this a transparent process President  Trump has the opportunity right now to  bring leaders around the table and  continue to have the leadership of the  United States has had on this issue and  really deal with the big problem of  elephants we just habitat loss and  large-scale commercial poaching I mean  this hunting issue is frankly a sideshow  and it's distracting attention away from  what is  really needed very quickly we mentioned  your virtual reality film my Africa tell  us what it's about  oh it's an amazing story spoken from the  from the perspective of symbaloo  communities from a from a young woman's  perspective of living with wildlife in  northern Kenya it's spectacular  everything from wildebeest crossing the  Maasai Mara to baby elephants and its  opening at Tribeca  and the wonderful Lupita the star of  Lupita is has lent her voice to it and  he does great job dr. engine thank you  so much for being with us this morning  thank you

